Let `--gpu_id -1` mean the CPU, as it does across GraFlag.

run.py sets `device = args.gpu_id` -- an int -- and moves the model, the
features and every buffer to it with `.to(device)`, so upstream as published
can only run on a GPU. In this image it has to run on the CPU: DGL
0.4.3.post1, the version upstream's README states, carries GPU machine code
for sm_30 to sm_70 only and no PTX, so on a newer GPU (this cluster's NVIDIA
A16 is sm_86) every DGL kernel launch fails without an error and leaves its
output at zero. Measured: one copy_src/sum message-passing step on the A16
returned all zeros (exp__ad_gcl__gad_cora__20261118_115058). See README.md.

For any `--gpu_id` >= 0 this line behaves exactly as upstream's, so the
patched tree runs upstream's own README commands unchanged.

Nothing else in run.py, model.py, aug.py or utils.py names a device: they
take `device` from here or use the tensors' own, and the two
torch.cuda.manual_seed calls are no-ops without a GPU.

Generated with `git diff` against the tree SOURCE_REF pins.

